WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.992
Grade evidence along several dimensions.

00:00:02.992 --> 00:00:07.972
Relevance asks whether the evidence addresses the user's information need.

00:00:07.972 --> 00:00:13.752
Sufficiency asks whether there is enough information to answer the complete question.

00:00:13.752 --> 00:00:20.167
Authority asks whether the source is approved and trustworthy for this kind of claim.

00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:24.164
Freshness asks whether the source is current enough.

00:00:24.779 --> 00:00:33.091
For our return example, a forum post about returns may be relevant, but it is not an authoritative company policy.

00:00:33.091 --> 00:00:39.091
An old company policy may be authoritative in origin but no longer current.

00:00:39.091 --> 00:00:44.836
A current return policy may still be insufficient without the order date.

00:00:44.836 --> 00:00:49.606
A simple binary grade can be useful when teaching the basic loop.

00:00:49.606 --> 00:00:53.329
But a structured grade gives more information when debugging.

00:00:53.779 --> 00:00:57.541
The key question is not just, is this document good?

00:00:57.541 --> 00:00:59.376
It is, good for what decision?
